/*Q28. Write a Java program to store and display the details of n vehicles using an array of objects.
Class: Vehicle
Create a class named Vehicle with the following instance variables:
• VehicleID (int): Stores the unique ID of the vehicle.
• Model (String): Stores the model name of the vehicle.
• Type (String): Stores the type of vehicle.
• Price (double): Stores the price of the vehicle.
Implement the following:
Parameterized Constructor
Create a constructor Vehicle(int VehicleID, String Model, String Type, double Price) that initializes all four instance variables using the
values passed as arguments and the this keyword.
Method
Implement display(), which prints the vehicle's ID, model, type, and price on a single line, separated by single spaces, in the following
format:
VehicleID Model Type Price
Use the default Java string representation of the double price.
Class: VehicleDetails
The VehicleDetails class should:
1. Read an integer n, representing the number of vehicles.
2. Create an array of Vehicle objects of size n.
3. Read each vehicle's ID (int), model (String), type (String), and price (double), in that order.
4. Create a Vehicle object for each record using the parameterized constructor and store it in the array.
5. Invoke display() for every vehicle in the array, preserving the input order.
Input Format
The first line contains an integer n, representing the number of vehicles.
The next n lines contain four values separated by spaces:
• An integer VehicleID
• Astring Model
• Astring Type
• Adouble Price
Output Format
• Display the details of each vehicle on a separate line in the following format:
VehicleID Model Type Price
• The values must be displayed in the same order as they were given in the input.
Constraints
• 1 ≤ n ≤ 100
• 1 ≤ VehicleID ≤ 10^6
• Model and Type contain no spaces.
• 0 < Price ≤ 10^9
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class Vehicle {
    int VehicleID;
    String Model;
    String Type;
    double Price;
 
    Vehicle(int VehicleID, String Model, String Type, double Price) {
        this.VehicleID = VehicleID;
        this.Model = Model;
        this.Type = Type;
        this.Price = Price;
    }
 
    void display() {
        System.out.println(VehicleID + " " + Model + " " + Type + " " + Price);
    }
}
 
public class VehicleDetails {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        Vehicle[] vehicles = new Vehicle[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            vehicles[i] = new Vehicle(sc.nextInt(), sc.next(), sc.next(), sc.nextDouble());
        }
        for (Vehicle v : vehicles) {
            v.display();
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}
